    <section class="notes" data-view-only="data" hidden>
      <h2>About the data</h2>
      <!-- Its contents, held beside the notes as the page scrolls, or in a bar under the tabs on a narrow
           screen; notes.ts lists the notes' bold leads and headings in it -->
      <div class="notes-layout">
      <nav class="notes-toc" id="notes-toc" aria-label="About the data, contents">
        <button type="button" class="notes-toc-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="notes-toc-list">
          <span class="notes-toc-label">Contents</span>
          <span class="notes-toc-current"></span>
        </button>
        <ol id="notes-toc-list"></ol>
      </nav>
      <div class="notes-body" id="notes-body">
      <p>
        <strong>Spending per pupil</strong> is the Massachusetts Department of Elementary and Secondary Education's
        (DESE) <a href="https://educationtocareer.data.mass.gov/d/er3w-dyti">per-pupil expenditure</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/districts-spending-per-pupil.csv" download>CSV</a>) from each district's end-of-year report, divided by in-district full-time-equivalent pupils (FTE) and grouped
        into the report's nine categories; its Other combines DESE's professional development and instructional
        materials, equipment, and technology. FY2005 to FY2008 come from DESE's workbooks for those years, which its
        Public Records Center provided in October 2026 in answer to public records request P004939-101625
        (<a href="https://github.com/timshores/schools/blob/main/ppe_report/data/dese_archive/README.md">the files, and
        how they were read</a>). It leaves out tuition for out-of-district FTE, the district's own pupils educated
        elsewhere, because, as DESE puts
        it, those costs "vary greatly depending on the reason for going out of district."
      </p>
      <p class="notes-sub">
        <strong>Spending on out-of-district FTE</strong> shows that tuition on its own, per out-of-district FTE pupil.
        DESE's data has no separate figure for it from FY2009, so the explorer works it out from DESE's totals: total
        spending less in-district spending. DESE printed the figure for FY2005 to FY2008, and the explorer's matches it
        there, but only because those years' in-district totals were themselves worked out from DESE's printed
        payments. So no DESE figure checks the method in the years that need it, from FY2009.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Enrollment</strong> comes in three measures. In-district FTE is full-year enrollment of students who
        live in the district or are choiced or tuitioned into it. Foundation enrollment counts the residents the
        district is financially responsible for on October 1 of the prior year. Out-of-district FTE counts the
        district's pupils educated elsewhere. In-district and out-of-district FTE come from
        <a href="https://educationtocareer.data.mass.gov/d/er3w-dyti">DESE's expenditure data</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/districts-spending-per-pupil.csv" download>CSV</a>), foundation enrollment from its
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/default.html">Chapter 70 district profiles</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/districts-chapter70.csv" download>CSV</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Chapter 70 aid and net school spending</strong> are per foundation pupil, from DESE's
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/default.html">Chapter 70 district profiles</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/districts-chapter70.csv" download>CSV</a>). The state sets a required
        level of net school spending for each district each year. Chapter 70 aid pays part of it, and the town, or a
        regional district's member towns, pays the required local contribution. Anything above that is a local choice. Because spending per pupil and
        net school spending use different denominators, the two charts are not the same measure: they are a picture
        of apples in the morning compared with a picture of apples in the evening. Both dollar charts share one scale.
        For FY2023, DESE's current file, from March 2026, gives 90 districts, most of them regional, another
        district's required or actual net school spending: Gill-Montague's figures are Hawlemont's, for example. Until DESE corrects it, the explorer takes those FY2023 figures from DESE's August 2025 file, listed
        under Source files below.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Trends</strong> are the straight line that best fits every year's value. Its growth is the yearly rate
        that carries the line from its first year to its last; because the line weighs every year, an unusual first or
        last year moves it less than it would move growth measured from the first year's value to the last year's.
        Where the line starts at or below zero, the rate is its yearly rise as a share of its average value. With a
        chart's Trends only dial on, the comparison's trend is drawn too, in the comparison's color.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Legends and dials.</strong> A chart's legend picks what it shows: a category or a line alone, or
        several. A line picked alone draws as a single measure does, with its trend and the comparison's line. Each
        chart's Comps dial shows or hides its comparison, and the Comps dial in the controls does it for every chart;
        picking a comparison turns it on everywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Correlations</strong> on the Commonwealth tab's plots are Spearman's ρ, which compares the districts'
        order on two measures: 1 when they fall in the same order, −1 when they fall in the opposite order, and near 0
        when there is no consistent order. Each pair is checked for the pitfalls Aggarwal and Ranganathan describe in
        <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5079093/">“Common pitfalls in statistical analysis: The use
        of correlation techniques”</a> (2016), and the plot lists any it falls into as cautions: the measures count the
        same thing, they are two views of the same thing, the pattern curves back, a few districts pull the result, one
        district moves it, the regions sit apart, the spread is uneven, there are few districts, or districts share
        towns. Two measures count the same thing, marked ◇ in the list of measures, when one is built from the other or
        both from the same number: Chapter 70 aid counts low-income students and English learners, because the
        foundation budget adds for each. Two views of the same thing share no number but measure one condition.
        Low-income students and income per capita both measure how much money families have, and many English
        learners are also students of color, so the same students count toward both shares. A strong ρ between such a
        pair is expected: it shows that the two measures agree, not something districts do. The plots shown first have
        a ρ at least 0.4 from zero, either way, and no caution but shared towns. A log scale can't place zero, so it leaves out the districts
        at zero on the measure across, and the plot's caption says how many.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Since 1990</strong> adds FY1990 to FY2004, from DESE's per-pupil reports in the same public records
        request. For those years the explorer draws what DESE called day-program spending, an older, narrower measure:
        what a district spent running its own schools, mainly from its school committee budget and the town's indirect
        costs for them, divided by the pupils enrolled there. It leaves out pupils sent to other schools and what is
        paid for them, so it usually runs below the columns. DESE's reports for some of those years also give an
        integrated cost per pupil, which counts the pupils a district pays for elsewhere; the explorer hasn't read it
        yet. It continues to FY2006, so the two measures overlap for
        two years: in FY2005, which DESE's workbook sets out both ways, Abington's was $6,766 per pupil against $7,463
        in the columns. The explorer draws it as a separate dashed line, and each measure
        has its own trend and growth rate. The older measure's averages weight each district by DESE's average
        membership from FY1997. DESE's reports give no pupils for FY1990 to FY1996, so those years use the fall
        enrollment the National Center for Education Statistics counts
        (<a href="https://educationdata.urban.org/">Common Core of Data</a>), a head count from a different agency; in
        FY1997 and FY1998, when both exist, nine districts in ten differ by less than 9%. Chapter 70 figures reach back
        to FY1993, DESE's first profile year. Districts that no longer exist, including towns that once ran their own
        schools, are listed under Former districts with the years they have figures for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Totals</strong>, under Amounts, turn the figures per pupil or per resident back into dollars. A
        district's spending and revenue per pupil are multiplied by its in-district FTE, and its Chapter 70 figures by
        its foundation enrollment. A town's figures are multiplied by its population. An average's totals are its
        average member's: the group's pooled figure times its average enrollment or population. A total moves with
        enrollment and population as well as with spending, so it can rise while spending per pupil falls.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Enrollment groups</strong> sort traditional districts by FY<span data-latest>2025</span> in-district FTE,
        using the boundaries <a href="/artifacts/western-ma-ppe-report-fy2025.pdf">the report</a> set from FY2024 Western MA
        enrollment (the report calls them cohorts): Tiny up to 200 FTE, Small 201–800, Medium 801–1,600, Large 1,601–10,000, and Very large above
        that. Vocational districts and charter schools are groups of their own. Regions are the six regions of the
        state's Executive Office of Health and Human Services. As the report left Springfield out of its Western MA average, region averages leave out the ten very
        large districts, which have an average of their own; together they teach 23% of the pupils in
        traditional districts. There are two statewide averages: Statewide, of every traditional district, and the same
        without the ten. Towns have the same two, with and without the ten cities those districts serve
        (<a href="#groups">groups and their members</a>). A
        region with fewer than three districts in a
        enrollment group has no regional average for it, so "its enrollment group in its region" uses the statewide group there. Spending
        averages are weighted by in-district FTE, Chapter 70 averages by foundation enrollment, and enrollment
        averages are per district. <a href="https://www.mass.gov/doc/gatewaycitiesdocx/download">Gateway Cities</a>
        are the 26 mid-size cities the Commonwealth lists, whose median household income and college attainment fall
        below the state's (the list dates from 2014). Their districts, and the cities on the Towns tab, have averages
        of their own, alongside their enrollment and population groups. Two virtual schools, the state's institutional schools, and the Massachusetts
        Academy of Math and Science are not included.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Community types</strong> follow the Commonwealth's own lines where it draws them. A traditional district
        is rural at 35 or fewer students per square mile, its foundation enrollment over its towns' land area: the test
        for DESE's <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/foundation/fy2026-rural-school-aid.html">rural school
        aid</a>, which also requires income below the state average. DESE's three tiers, 11 or fewer, over 11 to 21, and
        over 21 to 35, have averages of their own. A town is rural under 500 residents per square mile, the line in the
        <a href="https://berkshireplanning.org/wp-content/uploads/program_documents/brpc_initiative/massachusetts-rural-policy-plan/default/Rural_Policy_Plan_Launch_Presentation.pdf">Rural
        Policy Plan</a> for the Commonwealth's Rural Policy Advisory Commission. The Inner Core is Boston and its densest
        neighbors, the Metropolitan Area Planning Council's Metropolitan Core Communities and Streetcar Suburbs in its
        <a href="https://www.mapc.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Massachusetts-Community-Types-Summary-July_2008.pdf">community
        types</a>, as its <a href="https://datacommon.mapc.org/">DataCommon</a> profiles give them: Boston, Cambridge,
        Chelsea, Everett, Malden, Revere and Somerville, and Arlington, Belmont, Brookline, Medford, Melrose, Newton,
        Waltham, Watertown and Winthrop. The other 22 Gateway Cities are a type of their own; Chelsea, Everett, Malden
        and Revere count with the Inner Core, though the Gateway Cities comparison keeps all 26. The state has no name
        for the rest, called suburban and other here.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Income fifths</strong> rank the 351 towns by income per resident: the residents' income the Chapter 70
        formula uses for FY2027, from 2023 state tax returns, divided by the Census Bureau's population estimate for July
        2023. Each fifth holds 70 or 71 towns. A district's fifth ranks the combined income per resident of its towns
        among the 286 traditional districts, 57 or 58 to a fifth. Each fifth has an average of its own, for "Its income
        fifth" under Compare with. The cap tab ranks the towns the same way, for whichever year it shows. Land areas are the Census Bureau's
        (<a href="https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/GENZ2023/shp/cb_2023_25_cousub_500k.zip">2023 cartographic
        boundaries</a>); enrollment is DESE's latest foundation enrollment, and population the latest estimate. Every
        group and its members are listed under <a href="#groups">Groups and their members</a>, below, and in a
        file (<a href="/muni-spending/data/groups.csv" download>CSV</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Revenue by source</strong> comes from the Census Bureau's
        <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/school-finances.html">Annual Survey of School System Finances</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/districts-revenue.csv" download>CSV</a>, in dollars, each column naming the Census items it adds up),
        divided by in-district FTE like spending. It covers regional districts only: for many town-run
        districts, Census's local revenue is only part of what the town spends on its schools (for Amherst in FY2024,
        $18.4 million of revenue against $37.6 million of spending). The survey's Massachusetts file has no charter
        schools, so they and their average have no revenue figures. Payments from towns include a regional
        district's member-town assessments; payments from other school districts include tuition for pupils they
        send. Revenue per pupil usually runs about a third above spending per pupil, because it also pays for what
        the spending measure leaves out, such as tuition for out-of-district FTE, school buildings, and the
        teachers' pensions the state pays into the teachers' retirement system on districts' behalf. School building
        aid can arrive in one large payment. In FY2009 the state paid $412 million of Chapter 70 aid with federal
        stimulus money, so Census counts about a tenth of that year's Chapter 70 aid as federal. Census publishes
        each year's figures more than a year after it ends.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Special education</strong> spending comes from DESE's
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/research/radar/">Special Education Direct Expenditure Trends</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/districts-special-education.csv" download>CSV</a>, in dollars, with each district's
        spending in all), from the year-end financial reports behind its RADAR tools. It counts direct costs only: teaching, other
        instructional services, and transportation in the district's own schools; tuition at other public schools,
        collaboratives, private and out-of-state schools, and transportation to them; health services for pupils in
        private schools; and what grants and revolving funds paid. Per pupil divides by every FTE pupil the district is
        responsible for, in district and out, as DESE does for total spending per pupil. The state's payments back come
        from DESE's <a href="https://educationtocareer.data.mass.gov/d/ab34-d3ma">Circuit Breaker reimbursements</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/districts-circuit-breaker.csv" download>CSV</a>, by the year paid, as DESE gives
        it). The chart puts each payment with the year of the costs it repays: a year's claim is paid the next year, in
        quarterly payments and any supplement, and extraordinary relief in the same year. An average pools its
        districts' dollars over their pupils, and a district with no claim counts as repaid nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Budgets</strong> come from DESE's
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/compliance.html">net school spending compliance summaries</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/districts-nss-budgets.csv" download>CSV</a>, in dollars, with each file's
        address). Each year, on its End-of-Year Pupil and Financial Report, a district gives DESE what it spent in the
        year that just ended and what it has budgeted for the year under way, both as net school spending, the
        spending the state counts toward its minimum. DESE posts the summaries from FY2021 on; the earlier ones, from
        FY2010, are the Internet Archive's copies of its site. The budget is the one approved, not the school
        committee's first proposal; level-services budgets, the cost of the year's programs and staff carried into
        the next, are each district's own figure, and the state doesn't collect them. Per pupil divides by
        foundation enrollment, as the Chapter 70 chart does; actual vs. budget compares dollars of the day.
        An average pools its districts' budgets over their pupils, and their spending over their budgets.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Students</strong> come from DESE's
        <a href="https://educationtocareer.data.mass.gov/d/t8td-gens">enrollment by race and ethnicity, gender, and selected populations</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/districts-students.csv" download>CSV</a>), the counts on its school profiles:
        each district's students on October 1, shown at the fiscal year that school year falls in. Race and ethnicity
        are as families report them, Hispanic or Latino students of any race, and each race of the students who
        aren't. Low income has meant three things: eligibility for free or reduced-price meals through FY2014,
        economically disadvantaged, by families' enrollment in programs such as SNAP, from FY2015 to FY2021, and a
        broader low income from FY2022; the chart keeps them apart. An average pools its districts' students.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Town budgets</strong> come from each town's <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=ScheduleA.GenFund_MAIN">Schedule A</a>
        (CSVs: <a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-spending.csv" download>spending</a>, <a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-revenue.csv" download>revenue</a>, each with population), the year-end report of general fund revenue and
        spending that town accountants file with the state Division of Local Services. Enterprise, capital, and other
        funds are not included. Each town's school districts and FY2025 required local contributions come from DESE's
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2025/chapter-2025.html">Chapter 70 tables</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-school-districts.csv" download>CSV</a>), and each town takes the
        region of its own school district, or of its regional district. Dollars are per resident, by the population
        estimates below. They can also be shown per household, using the Census Bureau's
        <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs">American Community Survey</a> five-year estimates
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-households.csv" download>CSV</a>), or per taxable property, using the
        parcels of taxable real estate the assessors report (see Property, below), or in total. Each average pools its
        towns: their combined dollars over their combined residents, households, or taxable properties.
      </p>
      <p class="notes-sub">
        <strong>Population estimates</strong> are the Census Bureau's
        <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/popest.html">estimates</a> for July 1 at the start of each fiscal
        year. For FY2021 the April 1, 2020 estimate stands in, because the July 2020 estimate drops by more than a
        quarter in Amherst and recovers a year later. Population groups put each town with others its size, by the July
        2024 estimate: under 1,000 (26 towns), 1,000–2,499 (42), 2,500–9,999 (98), 10,000–24,999 (103), 25,000–49,999
        (55), 50,000–99,999 (18), and 100,000 or more (9). The ten cities whose school districts are very large, from
        Boston to Springfield, are in their population groups, but the regional town averages leave them out, as do the
        region averages of districts. So does the second statewide average; the first, Statewide, includes them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Property</strong> comes from the state Division of Local Services. Parcels and assessed values are
        each board of assessors' yearly <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=PropertyTaxInformation.LA4.Parcel_counts_vals">LA-4 report</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-property.csv" download>CSV</a>), as of January 1 before each fiscal
        year, grouped by the state's property use codes. Multifamily is two- and three-family homes and apartment
        buildings (codes 104, 105, 111–125). Other residential is mobile homes, lots with more than one house, and
        vacant or accessory residential land (103, 109, 130–132, 106). Mixed use is property with more than one use,
        such as a store with apartments above it (012–043). Farmland is land taxed under the Chapter 61A agricultural
        program; forest, recreation, and open space is land under Chapters 61 and 61B and class 2 open space. Personal
        property, such as business equipment and utility lines, is assessed but has no parcel. Exempt property, such
        as schools, churches, and public land, is left out. The
        <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=AverageSingleTaxBill.SingleFamTaxBill_wRange">average single-family tax bill</a>
        is the state's: single-family assessed value divided by single-family parcels, times the residential tax rate,
        set beside the Department of Revenue's income per capita. Amherst's FY2025 bill is 41% of its income per
        capita, the highest share in the state; income per capita counts every resident, students included.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The property tax levy</strong> is what a town raises from property taxes, and
        <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=Prop2.5.ExcessLevyCapandOverride_10_pres">Proposition 2½</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-levy.csv" download>CSV</a>) limits it. Each year a town's levy limit
        grows by 2.5% plus the tax on new construction, and voters can raise it with an override or exclude a debt
        from it; the charts' limit includes those exclusions. The limit itself can't pass the levy ceiling, 2.5% of the
        town's assessed value, though excluded debt can. Unused levy capacity is the gap between the limit and the levy a town sets: money it could
        raise without asking voters, and chooses not to. It can be read as tax relief, as careful budgeting, or as
        austerity. The tax rate here is the single-family bill over the home's value, which is the residential rate
        except where a town gives a residential exemption. The ceiling line and override capacity come from the same
        report: override capacity is the ceiling less the limit before debt exclusions, how much more an override could
        raise.
      </p>
      <p class="notes-sub">
        <strong>Proposition 2½ votes</strong>, listed under the levy chart, come from DLS's records of override,
        underride, debt exclusion, capital exclusion, and stabilization fund votes
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-prop25-votes.csv" download>CSV</a>), with the result and, where DLS has
        them, the amount and the count.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Reserves</strong> are each town's stabilization fund balances at June 30
        (<a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=Dashboard.Cat_1_Reports.StablPerBudget351">DLS</a>,
        <a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-stabilization.csv" download>CSV</a>) and the free cash the state certified from
        the town's balance sheet that day (<a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=Dashboard.Cat_1_Reports.CertifiedFreeCashBudget351">DLS</a>,
        <a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-free-cash.csv" download>CSV</a>), shown at the fiscal year that ended: the free
        cash certified as of July 1, 2026 is FY2026's.
      </p>
      <p class="notes-sub">
        <strong>Where free cash comes from</strong> is the Bureau of
        Accounts' proof of each town's free cash (<a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=BalanceSheet.FreecashProofComp">DLS</a>,
        <a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-free-cash-proof.csv" download>CSV</a>), FY2021 on: local receipts and state aid
        above or below their estimates, appropriations left unspent, and the year before's free cash not appropriated, less
        what the town is still owed and other adjustments, which add up to the free cash the state identifies. Their share of the budget divides by DLS's operating budget, the
        revenue the tax rate recap raises without enterprise or Community Preservation Act funds. A statewide comparison
        of each budget with what was spent isn't shown: Schedule A's spending leaves out transfers to stabilization and
        capital funds, which the budget includes, so the gap would count savings as money left unspent.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Long-term debt</strong> is the debt outstanding at each year's end, with general fund debt service
        (<a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=Dashboard.Cat_6_Reports.LongTermDebt351">DLS</a>,
        <a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-debt.csv" download>CSV</a>), and debt authorized but not yet borrowed
        (<a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=Dashboard.Cat_6_Reports.TotalAuthUnisDebt351">DLS</a>,
        <a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-authorized-debt.csv" download>CSV</a>); bond ratings are Moody's and S&amp;P's,
        as DLS lists them (<a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-bond-ratings.csv" download>CSV</a>).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Capital projects</strong> spending comes from Schedule A Part 4
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-capital-projects.csv" download>CSV</a>, with the funds' revenue).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Labor force</strong>, employment and the unemployment rate come from DLS's yearly figures
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-labor-force.csv" download>CSV</a>), which end with 2022.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Population by age, race and ethnicity</strong>, and the shares who are women and who have a disability,
        come from the Census Bureau's
        <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs">American Community Survey</a> five-year estimates, tables
        B01001, B03002 and B18101 (<a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-demographics.csv" download>CSV</a>, in counts), read
        from the Bureau's summary files. Estimates whose years overlap shouldn't be compared, so the charts use the four
        that don't, 2005–2009 through 2020–2024, each at the fiscal year after it ends. Eleven towns the Bureau has since
        given new codes have no 2005–2009 figures. Disability counts begin with 2008–2012.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Equalized valuation (EQV)</strong> is the state's estimate of the full and fair cash value of all
        taxable property in each city and town on January 1, set every two years by the Commissioner of Revenue
        (<a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=PropertyTaxInformation.EQV.EQV">EQVs since 1970</a>;
        <a href="/muni-spending/data/towns-eqv.csv" download>CSV</a>). Assessors value property at different
        distances from market prices, so the state's Bureau of Local Assessment compares each town's assessments
        with its recent sales, class by class, and divides each class's assessed value by that ratio: homes assessed
        at 95% of their sale prices have their value divided by 0.95. It then adds an estimate of new construction
        (<a href="https://www.mass.gov/info-details/equalized-valuations-eqv">DLS</a>;
        <a href="https://www.mass.gov/doc/eqv-leveling-the-playing-field/download">“EQV: Leveling the Playing Field”</a>).
        Chapter 70 uses EQV to measure each town's property wealth when it sets what the town should contribute. In
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2025/chapter-2025-whitepaper.docx">FY2025</a>, a
        town's target local contribution was 0.3906% of its January 2022 EQV plus 1.4312% of its residents' 2021
        income. The state chose the two percentages so that, statewide, property and income would each supply half
        of a total equal to 59% of all foundation budgets. No town's target could exceed 82.5% of its foundation
        budget, and {cappedFy2025} towns were at that cap. Each year a town's required contribution moves from the year before
        toward its target, and Chapter 70 aid pays at least the rest of the foundation budget.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Town and school budgets overlap without lining up.</strong> Education includes the town's own school
        department and what it pays regional and vocational school districts, so it does not match any one district's
        spending, and health insurance and pensions for a town's own school employees usually sit in fixed costs.
        State aid includes Chapter 70 aid for a town's own school district; aid to a regional district goes to the
        district. Intergovernmental assessments are the state and county charges on each town's Cherry Sheet, such as
        charter school and school choice tuition. Required local contributions are what the
        <a href="https://www.doe.mass.edu/finance/chapter70/fy2025/chapter-2025.html">Chapter 70 formula</a> sets
        for each town and district in FY2025.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Revenue groups</strong> combine <a href="https://dls-gw.dor.state.ma.us/reports/rdPage.aspx?rdReport=ScheduleA.GenFund_MAIN">Schedule A</a>'s eleven sources into six: charges, fees, and fines are
        service charges, licenses and permits, fines and forfeitures, and special assessments; other revenue is
        miscellaneous revenue and revenue from other governments; transfers and other financing are transfers and
        other financing sources. Figures are as each town filed them, and a few look like one-time entries: Lee's
        FY2024 revenue includes $27.3 million of miscellaneous revenue, more than half its total.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>FY<span data-latest>2025</span> dollars</strong> adjust for inflation with the Bureau of Labor
        Statistics' <a href="https://data.bls.gov/timeseries/CUUR0100SA0">consumer price index for the Northeast</a>
        (<a href="/muni-spending/data/cpi.csv" download>CSV</a>; CPI-U, series CUUR0100SA0), averaged over each July–June
        fiscal year. The report's
        <a href="/artifacts/western-ma-ppe-inflation-addendum-fy2025.pdf">inflation addendum</a> restates its growth
        rates against this index and against the federal price index for state and local government purchases.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Changed names.</strong> Until November 5, 2026, this explorer said "residents" for population, and
        "size peer groups" for both the districts' enrollment groups and the towns' population groups. The report
        calls the enrollment groups cohorts.
      </p>
      <GroupMembers />
      <h3 class="source-files-title" id="source-files">Source files</h3>
      <p>
        The agencies' own files behind this explorer are on GitHub, in the repository with the code that prepares
        them. Each file below links to its copy there, and each source links to the agency's page for it. The files
        keep the rows as each agency published them: the scripts that download them keep the Massachusetts rows of
        national files, stack years into one table, and add DESE's district codes. The repository's
        <a href={`${REPO_DATA}README.md`}>data README</a> names the script that downloads each file again.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="source-files">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">What it holds</th><th scope="col">Where it comes from</th><th scope="col">Retrieved</th></tr>
          </thead>
          {SOURCE_FILES.map((g) => (
            <tbody>
              <tr class="source-group"><th scope="rowgroup" colspan="4">{g.group}</th></tr>
              {g.files.map((f) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><a href={`${REPO_DATA}${f.file}`}>{f.file}</a></td>
                  <td>{f.holds}</td>
                  <td><a href={f.url}>{f.source}</a></td>
                  <td class="source-date">{f.retrieved}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          ))}
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The CSV links in the paragraphs above are this explorer's own files: the data for these districts and towns
        as each agency published it, except that the revenue file adds Census items up into the chart's sources, and
        every row links to its source. Built by Tim Shores with Claude Code. The code that prepares the data is at
        <a href="https://github.com/timshores/schools">github.com/timshores/schools</a>.
      </p>
      </div>
      </div>
    </section>

